1. Define the following commands:
git init
Creates a new Git repository in the current folder. It starts tracking the project using Git.
git add <file>
Adds a specific file to the staging area, preparing it to be committed.
git add .
Adds all new, modified, and deleted files in the current folder to the staging area.
git remote add origin <url>
Connects the local Git repository to a remote repository, usually on GitHub, GitLab, or Bitbucket.
git commit
Saves the staged changes permanently in the local repository with a message describing the changes.
git push
Uploads local commits to the remote repository so the changes are available online.

2.What is the difference between remote and local repositories?
A local repository is the Git repository stored on your own computer. You can edit files, commit changes, and view history even without internet.
A remote repository is the Git repository stored online, such as on GitHub, GitLab, or Bitbucket. It is used to back up code and share work with others.
Main difference:
Local repository = on your computer.
Remote repository = on the internet/server.
